{{History of Okaiken|periods}}
[[File:Sengoku period battle.jpg|thumb|right|300px|A 17th-century scroll depicting the Battle of Nakagute, a symbol of the collapse of traditional authority during the Sengoku era.]]

The {{nihongo|'''Sengoku period'''|戦国時代|Sengoku jidai|extra=lit. Age of Civil War; c. 1467 – 1625}} is a period in [[History of Okaiken|Sanese history]] marked by social upheaval, political intrigue, and nearly constant military conflict that lasted roughly from the middle of the 15th century to the beginning of the 17th century. The conflict had arisen from the structural failure of the shōen estate system imposed during the late [[Asama period]]. By the time the [[Heiji period]] concluded, the control of the shōen system had been overtaken by local provincial lords, known as daimyōs, and local religious groups such as Jōdo-shinshu.

Although the [[Minato Shogunate]] retained the structure of the Imperial Bakufu and instituted a warrior government based on social economic rights and obligations established by the [[Jofun Code]], it failed to win the loyalty of some daimyō, especially those whose areas were very far from the capital of Heiji-kyō. Prominent among these dissenters were the [[Uesugi clan]] headed by [[Uesugi Terutora]] and the [[Chosokabe clan]], headed by [[Chosokabe Morihiro]]. 

As trade with the [[Kingdom of Zong]] grew and the introduction of Sanese-[[Kwangju]] trade accelerated, the economy of the archipelago developed rapidly, and many commercial cities such as [[Toyozawa]] and [[Aizawa]] were established. This, combined with developments in agriculture and small-scale trading, led to the desire for greater local autonomy throughout all levels of the social hierarchy. As early as the beginning of the 16th century, the suffering caused by earthquakes such as the Great Joyu Earthquake of 1549 and widespread famines triggered several armed uprisings by farmers.

==Origins==
{{Main|Kitayama period|Schism of the Dual Suns}}

While the Sengoku period is connected to the events that saw the decentralization of the Kitayama Shogunate, strictly speaking, the period began in 1467 with the outbreak of the Taiju war. The shogunal government of the Minato shogunate, although in power for more than a century, was quickly losing its control of the government. This was partly due to the decaying shōen system and the increasing influence of rival clans upon the imperial family, which escalated regional conflicts. 

The geopolitical landscape of the archipelago had already been fractured during the 14th century by the Schism of the Dual Suns. This bitter succession war split the Imperial line, creating a Minato-backed Northern Court in Mito (modern Okaiken) and a Southern Court in Miyagami (modern [[Nakamizu]]). While the northern territories descended toward the Sengoku civil war, the southern nation of Nakamizu experienced its own localized fragmentation known as the Sengoku Ransei under the waning [[Nogizaka Shogunate]]. Meanwhile, the [[Yanbaru]] people to the far north remained completely detached from the escalating violence, maintaining a peaceful, independent maritime kingdom that profited immensely by acting as trade intermediaries between the warring southern domains and the Fosian mainland.

Additionally, by the time the war broke out between clans in 1467, the Minato clan leadership was busy promoting the arts and culture with the imperial family, the court, and the elite, effectively degrading their control over other sectors of the government. The Minato clan did propose some changes to the shōen system, primarily through the creation of another system that favored the Minato clan allies as well as the imperial court. This was known as the {{nihongo|Ritsuryō|律令|Ritsuryō}} system. This system was a series of laws inspired by Zongese Confucianism and legalism imported from the [[Fosia|Fosian mainland]]. This resulted in the confusing complexity of Ancient Sanese law that ultimately allowed ruling military governors of the clans, such as the Ogasawara and the Chosokabe, to usurp taxation power away from the Minato Shogunate.

===Governance and Fiscal Systems===
{{Main|Jofun Code|Kandaka system|Bunkoku-hō}}

The Sengoku period was defined not merely by military conflict but by a fundamental transformation in how territory was governed and taxed. The two competing fiscal systems of the era—the old [[Jofun Code]] and the emerging {{nihongo|''kandaka''|貫高|kandaka}} system—represented opposite philosophies of statecraft.

The Jofun Code assessed wealth in ''koku'' of rice, tying a daimyō's revenue and military obligation to agricultural yield. This system functioned adequately in the fertile plains of Harujima and the Inland Sea corridor, but proved disastrous for the volcanic provinces of [[Settsu]] and the maritime domains of [[Ishijima]], where rice cultivation was unreliable. Daimyō like [[Uesugi Terutora]] and [[Chōsokabe Morihiro]] were forced to pay taxes in a currency they could scarcely produce, turning the Jofun Code from a taxation framework into an extraction mechanism. The later Ritsuryō reform, which layered Confucian bureaucratic law atop the existing shōen structure, only worsened the confusion—creating overlapping jurisdictions between shōen holders, Ritsuryō officials, and military governors that no single authority could enforce.

The kandaka system, by contrast, assessed land not by area or rice yield but by its productive capacity in coin (''kan''). This innovation—adopted by the most commercially integrated domains—allowed sengoku daimyō to monetize their territories through trade tolls, market taxes, and maritime licensing rather than relying solely on agricultural extraction. The shift from ''koku'' to ''kan'' was not merely accounting; it was a revolution in governance. Lords who adopted kandaka could raise professional standing armies paid in coin, fund castle construction on a scale the Minato Shogunate never achieved, and conduct independent diplomacy with the [[Kingdom of Zong]] and other Fosian powers. However, the system also created extreme regional inequality: provinces near trade routes—the Inland Sea, [[Toyozawa]], [[Aizawa]]—grew wealthy, while landlocked agricultural provinces stagnated, intensifying the very rivalries that fueled the war.

At the local level, governance was surprisingly decentralized. Village headmen (''muramura-gashira'') managed irrigation, dispute resolution, and tax collection, serving as the real interface between the peasantry and the daimyō. In many domains, tax collection was outsourced to local notables or merchants—a practice known as tax farming—that created a class of intermediate power brokers neither samurai nor peasant. Each major daimyō also issued their own {{nihongo|domain law codes|分国法|bunkoku-hō}}, which functioned as constitutions for their territories. These covered criminal punishment, land inheritance, military obligation, and market regulation; the most sophisticated—the [[Ogasawara House Code]] and the [[Chōsokabe Maritime Code]]—were comprehensive legal frameworks that directly challenged the Minato Shogunate's claim to be the sole source of law.

===The Imperial Court During the Sengoku Period===
{{Main|Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Caged Mikado}}

Since the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]], the Emperor of Okaiken had been a {{nihongo|"Caged Mikado"|籠の帝|Kago no Mikado}}—a ceremonial figurehead whose primary function was to confer legitimacy on whichever warlord controlled the capital. By the Sengoku period, the Imperial Court's situation had deteriorated to near-total destitution. The court's annual income had plummeted from over 1,000 ''kan'' in the pre-Taiju era to approximately 690 ''kan'', as the shōen estates that once funded the throne were seized by daimyō, pirate fleets, or religious leagues. The court could no longer fund its own security or even the materials for essential Shoto rituals.

During the Taiju War, the Shogun and Emperor famously retreated to the Silver Pavilion of Daigo-ji and ignored the destruction of the capital—a moment that became emblematic of the court's helplessness. Yet the court was not entirely passive. It survived through three strategies: **title-granting** (conferring court ranks on daimyō in exchange for financial patronage), **decree-based mediation** (issuing ''mikiri'' to mediate between warring lords, providing a face-saving mechanism for daimyō who wished to end costly conflicts), and **cultural patronage** (maintaining the court as the arbiter of aesthetic standards that even the most ruthless warlord aspired to).

[[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]], who reigned during the most critical phase of the Sengoku period, embodied this paradox of ceremonial powerlessness combined with indispensable symbolic legitimacy. The [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558 shattered the convention that the emperor's person was inviolate; the Ogasawara clan's abduction of Prince Fumihito demonstrated that even the throne's physical safety was now subject to warlord power. Throughout the Ogasawara-Takigawa period, the emperor was used as a mediator and legitimizing instrument—Ogasawara Terumasa invoked imperial decrees to justify his campaigns, while Takigawa Kazumasu sought imperial sanction for the Sword Hunt and Land Survey. The court could not refuse; its survival depended on the patronage of whoever held the capital. Emperor Komei's single most significant act came in 1601, when he publicly recognized Honda Shigetada as his abducted son Prince Fumihito—a sovereign declaration that merged military authority with divine legitimacy and created the unprecedented {{nihongo|"Dual Sovereignty"|両界の令|Ryōkai-no-Rei}}.

==Taiju War==
{{main|Taiju War}}

The first event that sparked the Sengoku period in the northern archipelago was an upheaval called the Taiju war (1467–1477). Supporters of the Minato Shogunate were caught in a bitter succession crisis between three individuals set to replace the childless Minato no Yoshimoto in Mito. They were:
* Murakami Yoshihide of Ise, a nephew of Minato no Yoshimoto through his deceased brother, Minato no Yoshikatsu.
* Sugi Yoshimasa of Seto, a monk-turned-warrior and the younger brother of Yoshimoto.
* Katsuura Masayoshi of the Saitama domain, Yoshimoto's powerful cousin.

With the sickly Yoshimoto bedridden, he issued contradictory decrees. He publicly declared that Yoshimasa should be the next shogun of the realm; but privately, he also assured supporters that Yoshihide would be his next successor. Upon his death, utter confusion arose between the three different camps. The Sugi faction argued that Yoshihide should not be the next successor to the clan, proposing instead that Yoshimasa's young son, Sumiyoshi, inherit the title. 

Katsuura Masayoshi, on the other hand, took the confusion to his advantage and launched a preemptive attack on Murakami Yoshihide at his compound in Mito, killing him. This assassination angered Yoshihide's surviving supporters, who then launched an unprecedented assault on the residence of Masayoshi in Mito, killing Masayoshi's son in the process. Masayoshi then mistakenly blamed Yoshimasa for the death of his son, and he later waged an all-out war against the Katsuura and the Sugi clans. This brutal conflict of attrition led to the total destruction of the capital [[Mito]] and the areas surrounding it, sparking a century of nationwide warfare.

==Post-Taiju War and the Era of Gekokujō==
While the streets of the capital ran with blood, the Shogun and the Emperor famously detached themselves from reality, spending the decade-long war in the secluded Silver Pavilion of Daigo-ji. When the Treaty of Natsunaga finally ended the immediate conflict in 1474, the surviving clan armies simply abandoned the depopulated ruins of the capital to return to their ancestral lands, effectively ending the Minato Shogunate's relevance.

The power vacuum left by the ruined capital birthed the phenomenon of {{nihongo|Gekokujō|下克上}}, literally translated as "the low conquers the high." With the central authority impotent, the traditional prestige of court-appointed governors evaporated. They were ruthlessly overthrown by their own deputies, capable subordinates, or charismatic peasant leaders who rejected hereditary status in favor of meritocratic martial strength. 

One of the earliest examples was [[Hōjō Sōun]], a minor rōnin who seized Izu Province in 1493 and established the [[Late Hōjō clan]]. He transformed the Kanto region into a fortress state, ruling independent of any central authority for a century. In every province, the old guard fell; the prestigious Hosokawa clan was supplanted by their vassals, the Miyoshi, and the Toki clan was overthrown by the Saitō, who were in turn destroyed by their own vassal, the peasant-born general [[Takigawa Kazumasu]].

==The Rise of Religious Militancy==
The breakdown of order also radicalized the peasantry. In the absence of samurai protection, villages formed self-defense leagues known as ikki. The most powerful of these were the Ikkō-ikki (Single-Minded Leagues), radical adherents of Jōdo Shinshū Baiduism. 

Led by warrior-monks who preached that salvation could be earned through holy war against corrupt samurai, the Ikkō-ikki seized control of entire provinces. In Kaga Province, they overthrew the local daimyō and established a "Peasant's Kingdom" that ruled itself for nearly a century. Their fortified temple-cities, such as the massive Ishiyama Hongan-ji near Aizawa, became independent states within the state, posing a formidable challenge to any warlord attempting to unify the country. This religious militancy would eventually force the unifying warlords—Ogasawara, Takigawa, and Honda—to adopt policies of brutal suppression, culminating in the burning of Mount Hiei.

==Yuzawa Incidents and the Lost Prince==
{{Main|Yuzawa Incident|Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi}}

The chaos of the period eventually violated the sanctity of the Imperial House itself, an event that would paradoxically lead to the end of the Sengoku era. On October 12, 1558, while the Imperial Family was staying at the Mikasawa summer palace in Mino province, assailants breached the walls in what is now known as the Yuzawa Incident. The attack was orchestrated by the [[Ogasawara clan]], led by Ogasawara Terunobu, who sought to extort the Imperial Court for territorial concessions. During the raid, Prince Mutsuhito was assassinated, and the young Prince Fumihito, the son of [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]], was abducted.

The Ogasawara clan used the hostage prince to demand the northern quarter of Harujima island and a stipend of 300,000 koku, which the terrified court granted. However, rather than returning the prince, the Ogasawara falsely claimed he had died of disease. In reality, the young prince was abandoned at the Shōhei Temple, where he was raised as a commoner named "Ichimatsu" under the guidance of the monk Zen no Kishō. He was eventually discovered and adopted by [[Honda Shigetatsu]], the leader of the rising Honda clan. Unaware of his true lineage, the prince took the name [[Honda Shigetada]]. He would grow up to become a fearsome general known as "The Warrior who Surpassed Death," eventually reunifying the nation and reclaiming his birthright.

===Honda Shigetatsu and the Rise of the Honda Clan===
{{Main|Honda Shigetatsu|Honda clan}}

The adoption of the lost prince by [[Honda Shigetatsu]] was not merely an act of charity—it was the defining moment that would reshape the archipelago's fate. Shigetatsu was a provincial lord who embodied the Gekokujō ethos: he had risen through merit rather than birthright, and his governance of the Honda clan reflected a philosophy that valued ability over lineage. His decision to adopt "Ichimatsu" was characteristic of the Sengoku era's social mobility—a warlord could raise a commoner, a monk, or a foundling based on perceived potential, regardless of bloodline. The supreme irony was that the boy he raised as a commoner was the imperial heir, and the meritocratic ethos of Gekokujō accidentally restored the imperial bloodline it had displaced.

Shigetatsu laid the institutional foundations that the later Honda Shogunate would build upon. He shifted the Honda clan's revenue base from rice to the kandaka coin system, enabling the clan to fund professional soldiers and purchase arquebuses. He promoted officers based on competence rather than birth—a culture that would later produce both [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] and [[Honda Shigetada]] as generals under Ogasawara Terumasa. And unlike Ogasawara Terumasa, who ruled through terror, or Takigawa Kazumasu, who ruled through fear, Shigetatsu practiced **strategic patience**: building alliances, securing supply lines, and waiting for rivals to exhaust each other. This discipline was inherited by Shigetada, who spent years consolidating before the [[Battle of Tone]], and by Shigetomo, whose administrative state bore Shigetatsu's intellectual fingerprints in every institution from the Okami Rice Exchange to the meritocratic bureaucracy.

==Fall of the Minato Clan and Regional Warlords==
By the late 16th century, the Minato Bakufu was entirely defunct, functioning merely as a puppet to whichever regional warlord occupied the ruins of the capital. During this time, ambitious daimyō sought to march on the capital to claim national hegemony. 

In 1581, the powerful warlord [[Imazato Yoshimoto]] marched his massive army toward the capital. However, he was unexpectedly annihilated by a relatively minor daimyō named [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] at the [[Battle of Mazama]]. Terumasa did not just defeat Imazato; he reportedly displayed the severed heads of 3,000 enemy samurai along the Tokaido road, issuing a grim warning that the traditional rules of honorable warfare had been replaced by a new era of absolute destruction.

==Ogasawara Clan Rises to Power==
{{main|Ogasawara Terumasa}}
Ogasawara Terumasa spent the next decade dismantling the old medieval order with a brutality that earned him the moniker "The Demon King of the Sixth Heaven." Recognizing the threat posed by the religious leagues, Terumasa encircled their spiritual stronghold at Mount Hiei in 1584 and burned it to the ground, killing an estimated 20,000 warrior-monks and civilians. 

Having secured the capital and installed the final puppet Shogun, Minato Tadamoto, Terumasa broke the economic monopolies of the ancient guilds through his free-market edicts and instituted a strict meritocracy within his ranks. He promoted brilliant generals from the peasantry, most notably Takigawa Kazumasu and Honda Shigetada, expanding his territory rapidly until his sudden demise.

==Seto-Tosa Period==
{{Main|Seto-Tosa Period|Takigawa Kazumasu|Honda Shogunate}}

The final, apocalyptic phase of the Sengoku era is classified by historians as the Seto-Tosa Period (1582–1625). Ogasawara Terumasa's momentum seemed unstoppable until 1592, when he was betrayed by his most trusted general, Takigawa Kazumasu, during the Honto-ji Incident. Cornered in a burning temple, Terumasa committed seppuku, plunging the nation back into immediate chaos.

Takigawa Kazumasu moved with terrifying speed to fill the void, ruling through a mechanics of fear. Under his regime, the "Land Survey" was enforced at sword-point, and peasants were disarmed in the "Sword Hunt," freezing the social mobility that had defined the earlier Gekokujō era. However, Takigawa's reign was cut short when he was killed by a sniper during the siege of Katsuura in 1598.

The vacuum left by Takigawa triggered the bloodiest conflict of the era. The nation fractured into the Eastern Army, led by the lost prince Honda Shigetada, and the Western Army of Takigawa loyalists. This culminated on October 21, 1600, at the [[Battle of Tone]]. Involving over 160,000 men, massed arquebus volleys and critical battlefield defections led to the slaughter of 60,000 samurai and a total but pyrrhic victory for the Honda clan.

Following the victory, the ailing Emperor Komei publicly recognized Honda Shigetada as his abducted son, Prince Fumihito. In 1601, Shigetada was declared both Crown Prince and Sei-i Taishōgun, establishing a "Dual Sovereignty." The Sengoku period officially drew to a close following the Kinzō Incident and the subsequent Battle of Ichijoudani in 1625. The resulting "Great Reconciliation," combined with the looming external threat of the [[Kawaei clan]] in southern Nakamizu, forced the remaining daimyō to accept the rigid, authoritarian peace of the newly established [[Honda Shogunate]], ending over a century of civil war.

==Clans and Territories==
{{See also|List of daimyō of the Sengoku period|Fudai|Tozama}}

The Sengoku period fragmented the archipelago among dozens of competing clans, each governing their domains as de facto independent states. The major powers were distributed across the three islands as follows:

===Harujima===
The northernmost and most commercially developed island was the primary theater of the late Sengoku and Seto-Tosa periods. The [[Late Hōjō clan]], established by [[Hōjō Sōun]]'s seizure of Mino Province in 1493, ruled the Okazaki fortress region independently for a century. The [[Kojima clan]] controlled northern Harujima; Shigetada's secret truce with the Kojima secured his northern flank before the Battle of Tone. The [[Ii clan]], based in central Harujima, earned the nickname "The Red Devils" for their ferocity under [[Ii Naomasa]]; they became the vanguard of the Honda army after Shigetada's peace settlement, and their later revolt under [[Ii Naotaka]] would culminate in the Kinzō Incident.

===Ishijima===
The central island was the stronghold of the [[Ogasawara clan]], who had originally displaced the [[Uzumaki clan]] from the Masaki Peninsula in a Chain-Link War during the Hiraizumi-Daishin period. Under Ogasawara Terunobu and Terumasa, the clan expanded its territorial base dramatically across Ishijima and into Harujima. The [[Chōsokabe clan]], maritime lords of the southern Ishijima coast under [[Chōsokabe Morihiro]], were among the earliest and most persistent dissenters against the Minato Shogunate's rice-based taxation; they surrendered to the Honda in 1610 following a naval blockade. The [[Asakura clan]] and [[Azai clan]] of northern Ishijima were allies of the Ikkō-ikki who were destroyed by Ogasawara Terumasa.

===Yagikoshu===
The volcanic southern island was the domain of the [[Uesugi clan]], headed by [[Uesugi Terutora]], whose dissent from the Minato Shogunate was driven by the Jofun Code's rice-based taxation of non-rice-producing territories. The [[Shimazu clan]] of southern Yagikoshu surrendered to the Honda-Shogunate in 1612, two years after the Chōsokabe. The [[Hōsō clan]] of northern Harujima held out until 1618, when combined Imperial-Shogunate forces completed the military reunification.

===Trans-Regional Powers===
The [[Ikkō-ikki]] were not a clan but a theocratic confederation that controlled Kaga Province, the Ishiyama Hongan-ji near Aizawa, and Nagashima in Ise through a network of fortress-temples that spanned all three islands. The [[Kotsuna clan]] and the maritime branches of the [[Taira clan]] operated as pirate-subsidizers from the Inland Sea of Mitoyagi, fielding private naval forces that the Minato Shogunate was powerless to suppress. The [[Minato clan]], the former ruling house, was reduced to puppet status by the late 16th century; the final puppet Shogun, [[Minato Tadamoto]], was installed by Ogasawara Terumasa.

===Post-Unification Classification===
After the [[Battle of Tone]] (1600) and the establishment of the Honda Shogunate, the surviving clans were categorized by their loyalty to the new regime. The {{nihongo|''fudai''|譜代|fudai}} (hereditary vassals) who had supported the Honda before Tone—such as the Ii, Hotta, and Okudaira—were granted strategic domains in central Harujima and key government posts. The {{nihongo|''tozama''|外様|tozama}} (outsider lords) who submitted only after unification—including the Shimazu, Chōsokabe, and many former Ogasawara vassals—were relegated to the distant provinces of Settsu and the Ostrovs. The Ii clan, following the Kinzō Incident and Naotaka's seppuku, were pardoned but demoted to the smaller domain of Kozuka, where they served as a vital buffer against potential southern aggression for the next 250 years.

